<div class="diagram-notes">
<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px"><span style="font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace; font-size: 12px; color: var(--accent)">1 · Read-only boundary</span><p style="margin: 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.5; color: #2E2B26">check_control is the only tool that reaches a tenant's own infrastructure, and it only ever reads. Nothing in this system writes back to a customer's account.</p></div>
<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px"><span style="font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace; font-size: 12px; color: var(--accent)">2 · Single point of failure</span><p style="margin: 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.5; color: #2E2B26">Retrieval depends on one local Ollama instance. If it's down, it silently returns zero hits instead of erroring, rather than failing loud.</p></div>
<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px"><span style="font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace; font-size: 12px; color: var(--accent)">3 · Retry, not a second agent</span><p style="margin: 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.5; color: #2E2B26">A rejected finding goes back into the same loop as a retry. There's one agent, not a reviewer agent checking a writer agent.</p></div>
<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px"><span style="font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace; font-size: 12px; color: var(--accent)">4 · Two entry points, one loop</span><p style="margin: 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.5; color: #2E2B26">A browser request and the daily cron sweep both end at run_agent() with no branching in between.</p></div>
</div>
